---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";

const title = "ROE vs ROCE — What Each Measures and When They Disagree";
const description =
  "Return on equity and return on capital employed: the formulas, why a company " +
  "can show high ROE and poor ROCE, and how leverage inflates one but not the other.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description} crumb="Fundamentals / ROE and ROCE">
  <h1>ROE and ROCE</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Both measure how efficiently a company turns capital into profit. They differ
    in <strong>whose</strong> capital they count — and that difference is exactly
    where the useful information lives.
  </p>

  <h2>The formulas</h2>
  <div class="formula">ROE  = Net profit / Shareholders' equity
ROCE = EBIT / (Total assets − Current liabilities)</div>
  <p>
    ROE asks: for every rupee the owners put in, how much profit came back? ROCE
    asks: for every rupee employed in the business — owners' <em>and</em> lenders'
    — how much operating profit did it produce?
  </p>
  <p class="lede">
    Note ROCE uses <span class="c">EBIT</span>, before interest. That is
    deliberate. Since the denominator includes borrowed money, the numerator must
    include the profit earned on it, before any is paid away as interest.
  </p>

  <h2>Why leverage inflates ROE but not ROCE</h2>
  <p>
    This is the whole reason to look at both. Take an illustrative business
    earning ₹20 of operating profit on ₹100 of capital employed — a 20% ROCE.
  </p>
  <div class="formula">Funded entirely by equity:
  equity ₹100, debt ₹0
  EBIT ₹20, interest ₹0, tax @25% → net profit ₹15
  ROE  = 15/100 = 15%      ROCE = 20/100 = 20%

Funded half by debt at 10%:
  equity ₹50, debt ₹50
  EBIT ₹20, interest ₹5, tax @25% → net profit ₹11.25
  ROE  = 11.25/50 = 22.5%  ROCE = 20/100 = 20%</div>
  <p class="lede">
    ROE jumped from 15% to 22.5% while the business did not become one bit better
    at its actual job. ROCE stayed at 20% because it measures the operation, not
    the financing decision. <strong>High ROE alongside mediocre ROCE usually means
    leverage, not quality.</strong>
  </p>
  <p>
    That is fine while things go well. It also means a downturn hits the equity
    holder far harder, because the interest bill does not shrink when profit does.
    See <a href="/learn/debt-leverage/">debt and leverage</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the numbers</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>compare_roce_with_borrowing_cost</code><span class="meta">the key test</span></div>
      <p>If ROCE is below what the company pays to borrow, every additional rupee
        raised destroys value. Growth in that situation makes shareholders poorer,
        not richer. The comparison matters far more than any absolute threshold.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>consistency_over_level</code><span class="meta">what to look for</span></div>
      <p>A steady 18% across five years says more than one spectacular year at 40%
        followed by four at 8%. Look at the series, not the latest figure.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>sector_context</code><span class="meta">comparisons only work within a sector</span></div>
      <p>Asset-light businesses — software, consumer brands — structurally earn
        higher returns on capital than steel or infrastructure. Comparing across
        sectors tells you about the industry, not the management.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>banks_are_different</code><span class="meta">use ROA and ROE</span></div>
      <p>For lenders, debt <em>is</em> the raw material, so "capital employed" does
        not mean the same thing. Banks and NBFCs are judged on return on assets
        alongside ROE, with capital adequacy considered separately.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2>Breaking ROE apart</h2>
  <p>
    The DuPont decomposition splits ROE into three drivers, which tells you
    <em>why</em> it is what it is:
  </p>
  <div class="formula">ROE = (Net profit / Revenue)      ← net margin
    × (Revenue / Total assets)   ← asset turnover
    × (Total assets / Equity)    ← leverage</div>
  <p class="lede">
    Two companies can post identical ROE for completely different reasons. A
    premium brand gets there on margin; a distributor gets there on turnover; a
    leveraged infrastructure company gets there on the third term alone. Only the
    third is fragile.
  </p>

  <h2>Where these can mislead</h2>
  <div class="defs">
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>buybacks_shrink_equity</code><span class="meta">mechanical effect</span></div>
      <p>Buying back shares reduces the denominator, so ROE rises without any
        improvement in operations.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>old_written_down_assets</code><span class="meta">flattering</span></div>
      <p>A decades-old plant carried at near-zero book value makes capital employed
        look tiny and ROCE look superb. Replacing it at today's cost would tell a
        very different story.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>one_off_profits</code><span class="meta">check the P&L</span></div>
      <p>A large asset sale inflates net profit and therefore ROE for one year.
        Strip exceptional items out before drawing conclusions.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="def">
      <div class="h"><code>year_end_vs_average</code><span class="meta">method matters</span></div>
      <p>Some sources use closing equity, others the average of opening and
        closing. For a fast-growing company the two differ materially — make sure
        you are comparing like with like.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="ad">Advertisement</div>

  <h2>Where to find them</h2>
  <p class="lede">
    Neither is reported directly. Both are computed from the balance sheet and
    the profit and loss statement, which is why
    <a href="/learn/reading-financials/">reading the statements</a> comes first.
    Screening sites publish them, but the definitions vary — particularly whether
    equity is averaged, and whether ROCE uses EBIT or profit before tax. If a
    number looks surprising, recompute it yourself.
  </p>

  <p class="lede">
    Next: <a href="/learn/debt-leverage/">debt and leverage</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/cash-flow/">cash flow vs profit</a> ·
    <a href="/learn/evaluating-a-business/">evaluating a business</a>
  </p>
</Base>
